# Cogito reverse-proxy config — split-horizon realm design.
#
# Two vhosts, each independently trusted by the app via the X-Cogito-Realm
# header that THIS file stamps (Caddy's `header_up` with a value is a Set,
# which replaces any value a client tried to forge — so each block below is
# sufficient on its own). Do NOT add a `header_up -X-Cogito-Realm` "strip"
# anywhere: Caddy applies deletes after sets, so it would wipe the value the
# set just added and every client would fall back to realm=public.
#
#   cogito.home.arpa        — LAN only, never tunneled. Grants host privileges.
#   {$COGITO_PUBLIC_DOMAIN} — public, reached via Cloudflare Tunnel. Gated by
#                             the app's per-session join code, not by Caddy.
#
# Run standalone via docker-compose.yml next to this file (local testing or
# turnkey production), or copy the two site blocks below into a Caddy
# instance you already run elsewhere for other services — see README.md
# ("Already running your own Caddy?"). If merging into an existing Caddyfile
# that already has its own global options `{ ... }` block, drop the one below
# instead of having two.

{
	# .home.arpa can't get a public ACME cert, so both vhosts use Caddy's
	# internal CA — cloudflared connects to the public one with TLS
	# verification disabled, so a self-signed cert there is fine. Don't
	# auto-redirect :80 -> :443 so a plain curl smoke test still works.
	auto_https disable_redirects
}

# LAN — never in the Cloudflare Tunnel ingress. Host reaches this directly.
cogito.home.arpa {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy cogito:3000 {
		header_up X-Cogito-Realm lan
	}
}

# Public — reached only through the cloudflared container on cogito-net.
# Cloudflare's edge holds the publicly-trusted cert your friends actually
# see; the `tls internal` cert here is only seen by cloudflared itself.
{$COGITO_PUBLIC_DOMAIN} {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy cogito:3000 {
		header_up X-Cogito-Realm public
	}
}

# Note: Caddy proxies WebSocket upgrade requests (Socket.IO) automatically
# through reverse_proxy — no separate config needed — and header_up applies
# to those upgraded connections the same as normal HTTP requests.
